i. Process Control Commands
fork: This is a system call in programming (not a direct shell command) used in C/C++ to create a new process by duplicating the current process.

getpid: System call that returns the Process ID (PID) of the current process.

ps: Displays information about active processes. Example: ps aux shows all running processes.

kill: Sends a signal to terminate or control a process. Example: kill 1234 sends a termination signal to the process with PID 1234.

sleep: Delays execution for a specified number of seconds. Example: sleep 5 pauses for 5 seconds.

ii. Communication Commands: Input-Output Redirection and Pipe
Input-output redirection uses symbols to redirect input or output:

> redirects output to a file (overwrites).

>> appends output to a file.

< redirects input from a file.
Example: ls > filelist.txt saves directory list to a file.

Pipes (|) connect the output of one command to the input of another.
Example: ps aux | grep bash filters processes involving "bash".

iii. Protection Management Commands
chmod: Changes file or directory permissions. Example: chmod 755 file sets read/write/execute for owner, read/execute for group and others.

chown: Changes file owner. Example: chown user file

chgrp: Changes group ownership of a file. Example: chgrp groupname file
